A sorted map makes the greedy choice easy. The first key is always the smallest card still unused, so it has to start the next group. I remove one card from each required value, then repeat until the map is empty.

The input vector is not changed here. Also, the hand size must be divisible by `groupSize` before any grouping can work.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isNStraightHand(vector<int>& hand, int groupSize) {
        int n = hand.size();
        if (groupSize <= 0 || n % groupSize != 0) {
            return false;
        }

        map<int, int> count;
        for (int card : hand) {
            count[card]++;
        }

        while (!count.empty()) {
            int start = count.begin()->first;

            for (int value = start; value < start + groupSize; value++) {
                auto it = count.find(value);
                if (it == count.end()) {
                    return false;
                }

                it->second--;
                if (it->second == 0) {
                    count.erase(it);
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

For example, with `[1,2,3,6,2,3,4,7,8]` and size `3`, the groups start at `1`, then `2`, then `6`. Every count reaches zero.

If the hand is empty, the divisibility check passes and the map is already empty, so the result is `true`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

I used a frequency dictionary and a min-heap containing the distinct card values. The heap tells us where the next group must begin. After using a card, its count is reduced, and it is removed from the heap when no copies remain.

This keeps duplicate cards separate through their counts instead of putting duplicate values into the heap. The original list is left unchanged.

```python
import heapq

class Solution:
    def isNStraightHand(self, hand, groupSize):
        if groupSize <= 0 or len(hand) % groupSize != 0:
            return False

        counts = {}
        for card in hand:
            counts[card] = counts.get(card, 0) + 1

        min_heap = list(counts.keys())
        heapq.heapify(min_heap)

        while min_heap:
            start = min_heap[0]

            for value in range(start, start + groupSize):
                if counts.get(value, 0) == 0:
                    return False

                counts[value] -= 1
                if counts[value] == 0:
                    if min_heap[0] != value:
                        return False
                    heapq.heappop(min_heap)

        return True
```

A group size of `1` works automatically because each card is removed by itself. A size larger than the hand fails through the divisibility check or when a needed value is missing.

Time complexity: `O(n log u)`, where `u` is the number of different cards.
Space complexity: `O(u)`.

The smallest remaining value cannot be placed later in a valid group, so I always use it as the beginning of a group. `TreeMap` keeps the values sorted and also stores how many copies are left.

For a starting value `x` with count `c`, the code needs at least `c` copies of every value from `x` through `x + groupSize - 1`. Subtracting all `c` copies at once is a little shorter than building each duplicate group separately.

```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

class Solution {
    public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int groupSize) {
        if (groupSize <= 0 || hand.length % groupSize != 0) {
            return false;
        }

        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int card : hand) {
            counts.put(card, counts.getOrDefault(card, 0) + 1);
        }

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            int start = entry.getKey();
            int copies = entry.getValue();

            if (copies == 0) {
                continue;
            }

            for (int offset = 0; offset < groupSize; offset++) {
                int value = start + offset;
                int available = counts.getOrDefault(value, 0);
                if (available < copies) {
                    return false;
                }
                counts.put(value, available - copies);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Dry run for `[1,2,2,3,3,4]` with size `3`: value `1` has one copy, so one group uses `[1,2,3]`. Then value `2` has one copy left, which forms `[2,3,4]`.

The array is not sorted or modified. The map handles the ordering instead.

Time complexity: `O(n log u + u * groupSize)`.
Space complexity: `O(u)`.

I kept the actual cards in a `multiset`, so duplicates are stored and the smallest unused card is always available at `begin()`. After taking that smallest card, `find` locates each next consecutive value and removes one copy.

This is close to directly simulating the grouping rule. It is easy to follow, but each lookup and erase costs logarithmic time. The input vector itself is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isNStraightHand(vector<int>& hand, int groupSize) {
        int n = hand.size();
        if (groupSize <= 0 || n % groupSize != 0) {
            return false;
        }

        multiset<int> cards(hand.begin(), hand.end());

        while (!cards.empty()) {
            int current = *cards.begin();
            cards.erase(cards.begin());

            for (int used = 1; used < groupSize; used++) {
                int needed = current + used;
                auto it = cards.find(needed);
                if (it == cards.end()) {
                    return false;
                }
                cards.erase(it);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

An important failure case is `[1,2,4,5,6,7]` with group size `3`. The first group needs `3`, so the function returns `false` instead of using a non-consecutive value.

When `groupSize` is `1`, the loop simply removes one smallest card at a time.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the multiset.
